    <div class="questions-container">

        <!-- QUESTION 01 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 01
            </div>

            <h2>
                What is the main problem solved by using a database instead of
                an in-memory collection?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>It makes C# code shorter</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>It prevents the application from restarting</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>It allows data to persist after the application stops</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>It removes the need for MVC</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: C</strong>
                <p>
                    A database stores data persistently, so information remains
                    available even after the application stops or restarts.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 02 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 02
            </div>

            <h2>
                Which approach is being used when an existing database is used
                to generate EF Core entity classes?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>Code-First</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>Database-First</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>Model-First</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>Controller-First</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: B</strong>
                <p>
                    Database-First starts with an existing database and generates
                    EF Core entity classes and a DbContext from it.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 03 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 03
            </div>

            <h2>
                What is the primary purpose of Entity Framework Core?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>To create HTML pages automatically</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>To replace the MVC Controller</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>To map objects in code to relational database data</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>To replace the C# compiler</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: C</strong>
                <p>
                    Entity Framework Core is an ORM that allows .NET objects
                    and classes to work with relational database data.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 04 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 04
            </div>

            <h2>
                Which EF Core component is primarily responsible for
                communicating with the database?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>DbContext</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>DbSetView</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>ControllerContext</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>RazorContext</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: A</strong>
                <p>
                    DbContext manages the connection and communication between
                    the application and the database.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 05 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 05
            </div>

            <h2>
                What does the following command primarily do?
            </h2>

            <pre class="code-box">dotnet ef dbcontext scaffold "ConnectionString"
Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer -o Models</pre>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>Deletes the database</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>Creates a new MVC project</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>Generates EF Core models and a DbContext from an existing database</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>Starts the MVC application</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: C</strong>
                <p>
                    The scaffold command reads an existing database and generates
                    EF Core entity classes and a DbContext.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 06 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 06
            </div>

            <h2>
                Where is a database connection string commonly stored in an
                ASP.NET Core MVC application?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>Program.cs only</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>appsettings.json</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>Index.cshtml</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>Student.cs</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: B</strong>
                <p>
                    Connection strings are commonly stored in appsettings.json
                    in ASP.NET Core applications.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 07 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 07
            </div>

            <h2>
                A Student belongs to exactly one Section, while a Section can
                contain many students. What type of relationship is this?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>One-to-One</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>One-to-Many</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>Many-to-Many</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>Many-to-One only</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: B</strong>
                <p>
                    One Section can have many Students, while each Student
                    belongs to one Section. This is a one-to-many relationship.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 08 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 08
            </div>

            <h2>
                In the following example, what is SectionId?
            </h2>

            <pre class="code-box">public int SectionId { get; set; }

public Section Section { get; set; }</pre>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>Primary key of Student</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>Foreign key referencing Section</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>Navigation property</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>Database connection string</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: B</strong>
                <p>
                    SectionId acts as a foreign key connecting the Student
                    entity to the related Section entity.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 09 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 09
            </div>

            <h2>
                What is the purpose of a navigation property such as
                public Section Section { get; set; }?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>It stores the database password</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>It represents a relationship to another entity</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>It creates a new database</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>It validates the student's name</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: B</strong>
                <p>
                    A navigation property represents a relationship between
                    entities and allows related data to be accessed.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 10 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 10
            </div>

            <h2>
                What does .Include() generally allow EF Core to do?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>Delete the Section table</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>Load related Section data together with Students</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>Create a new Student</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>Validate Student input</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: B</strong>
                <p>
                    Include() tells EF Core to load related navigation property
                    data along with the main entity.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 11 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 11
            </div>

            <h2>
                Why might a ViewModel be used when displaying Student and
                Section information?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>To replace the database</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>To combine or shape the data specifically needed by the view</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>To automatically create database tables</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>To prevent controllers from using LINQ</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: B</strong>
                <p>
                    A ViewModel allows the application to provide the view with
                    only the data and structure it needs.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 12 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 12
            </div>

            <h2>
                Consider this query:
            </h2>

            <pre class="code-box">var students = _context.Students
    .Include(s => s.Section)
    .ToList();</pre>

            <h2>
                What is the main benefit of Include(s => s.Section)?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>It loads the related Section navigation property</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>It creates a Section object manually</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>It removes the foreign key</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>It prevents the query from accessing the database</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: A</strong>
                <p>
                    Include(s => s.Section) loads the related Section data
                    together with the Student records.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 13 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 13
            </div>

            <h2>
                Which type of validation occurs in the browser before a
                request is sent to the server?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>Database-level validation</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>Client-side validation</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>Server-side validation</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>EF Core migration validation</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: B</strong>
                <p>
                    Client-side validation occurs in the browser before the
                    form is submitted to the server.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 14 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 14
            </div>

            <h2>
                Why is server-side validation still necessary if client-side
                validation exists?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>Client-side validation can be bypassed</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>Client-side validation automatically modifies the database</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>Server-side validation only works with SQLite</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>Client-side validation cannot display messages</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: A</strong>
                <p>
                    Client-side validation can be bypassed, so server-side
                    validation is needed to protect the application and data.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 15 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 15
            </div>

            <h2>
                A school requires every student to have a unique Student
                Number. Which rule best represents this requirement?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>Student Number should always be nullable</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>Student Number should be unique</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>Student Number should always be the same</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>Student Number should contain only spaces</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: B</strong>
                <p>
                    A unique constraint ensures that no two students use the
                    same Student Number.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 16 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 16
            </div>

            <h2>
                Which is the best reason for having a database-level unique
                constraint on StudentNumber?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>It protects data integrity even if application-level validation is bypassed</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>It makes Razor Views render faster</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>It removes the need for a Controller</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>It automatically creates a ViewModel</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: A</strong>
                <p>
                    A database-level constraint provides an additional layer
                    of protection for data integrity.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 17 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 17
            </div>

            <h2>
                What is the purpose of a try...catch block in a controller?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>To create navigation properties</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>To catch and handle exceptions that may occur during execution</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>To generate database tables</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>To perform client-side validation</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: B</strong>
                <p>
                    A try...catch block allows the application to catch and
                    handle exceptions instead of allowing them to crash the request.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 18 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 18
            </div>

            <h2>
                Which middleware is commonly used in ASP.NET Core for
                centralized exception handling?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>UseDatabase()</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>UseExceptionHandler()</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>UseValidationHandler()</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>UseMvcDatabase()</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: B</strong>
                <p>
                    UseExceptionHandler() is commonly used for centralized
                    exception handling in ASP.NET Core.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 19 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 19
            </div>

            <h2>
                A user requests /Student/999, but Student 999 does not exist.
                What would be the most appropriate response?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>Display the student's information anyway</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>Display a Not Found (404) response/page</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>Delete Student 999</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>Create Student 999 automatically</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: B</strong>
                <p>
                    When a requested resource does not exist, the appropriate
                    HTTP response is Not Found, or 404.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- QUESTION 20 -->
        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-label">
                QUESTION 20
            </div>

            <h2>
                A student already belongs to Section A for a particular
                subject. The application attempts to assign the same student
                to Section A again. What is the primary concern?
            </h2>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span>A</span>
                    <p>Data integrity</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>B</span>
                    <p>HTML formatting</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>C</span>
                    <p>CSS inheritance</p>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span>D</span>
                    <p>Razor syntax</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>✓ Correct Answer: A</strong>
                <p>
                    Preventing duplicate assignments protects the correctness
                    and integrity of the application's data.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


